    "narrative_summary": "The dominant narrative is that U.S. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent has launched active buybacks/interventions in the long‑end Treasury market to try to cap rising yields, triggering a cascade of market reactions — rising gold and bitcoin, dollar weakness, and persistent investor skepticism. Described as an open system, the intervention injects funds and signals into the Treasury market and interacts with feedback loops linking yields, inflation expectations, Fed credibility (and its Jackson Hole moment), FX and safe‑haven demand, market liquidity, and fiscal financing choices; disturbances propagate to global asset prices and policy credibility and may prompt further policy adjustments. The system boundary covers Treasury actions, bond market microstructure and participants, short‑ and long‑term yield formation, and immediate spillovers into FX, precious metals and crypto; external inputs (global demand for safe assets, Fed policy, economic data) enter as driving forces.",
    "scope": "Inside: U.S. Treasury market (long‑term yields, auctions, buybacks, Treasury General Account), Treasury policy and announcements (Bessent), market participants and liquidity providers, investor expectations, Fed signals and speeches (e.g. Warsh), immediate spillovers to dollar, gold and crypto prices, and short‑term market functioning.",
